\fBskill\fR sends a signal to processes selected by tty, user, PID,
or command name. It exists primarily for compatibility with shell
scripts written for the legacy procps tool of the same name; for
new code, \fBpkill\fR(1) offers a richer matching language and
clearer flag conventions.
.PP
The signal can be specified in three ways:
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\-\-signal\fR \fISIG\fR — preferred, unambiguous form.
\fISIG\fR is a name (\fBKILL\fR, \fBHUP\fR), a name with the
\fBSIG\fR prefix (\fBSIGKILL\fR), or a number (\fB9\fR).
.IP \(bu 2
A short \fB\-\fR\fISIG\fR flag — \fBskill \-KILL bash\fR,
\fBskill \-9 bash\fR, \fBskill \-SIGKILL bash\fR. The flag may
appear at any position on the command line; only the first
recognised signal name or number is consumed as the signal.
.IP \(bu 2
Default: \fBTERM\fR (15) when no signal is specified.
.PP
Matching uses positive filters: a process must satisfy at least
one filter, and it must satisfy all of the supplied filter kinds.
Bare positional PIDs (\fBskill 1234 5678\fR) are also accepted.
With \fB\-n\fR (\fB\-\-no\-action\fR) \fBskill\fR prints matching
PIDs instead of signalling — the safe way to vet a destructive
invocation.
.PP
\fB\-l\fR / \fB\-L\fR list the supported signal names (alphabetical
flat list and a tab-separated number+name table, respectively),
matching the behaviour of \fBkill\fR(1) so the listings can be
cross-referenced.
